Purpose-Aims to develop, conceptualize the notion of sustainable global careers by integrating insights from sustainable career theory, global mobility research, sustainable human resource management, addressing the existing theoretical gap by explaining how global career trajectories can remain sustainable over time while balancing individual well-being, organizational performance, societal and environmental responsibilities.&nbsp; 
<div>
 Design/methodology/approach-Adopts a conceptual research design based on an extensive review and synthesis of existing literature on sustainable careers, global careers, expatriate management, sustainable HRM. Using the Ability-Motivation-Opportunity (AMO) theory as a guiding framework, an integrated conceptual model is developed.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Findings-Proposes a comprehensive framework for understanding sustainable global careers as dynamic, adaptive sequences of international work experiences that promote long-term happiness, health, productivity. Highlights the critical role of HRM practices in enhancing global careerists' abilities, motivation, opportunities, while emphasizing the influence of host-country context, cross-cultural competencies, proactive individual behavior. Also identifies conditions that may lead to non-sustainable global careers, including adjustment challenges, career shocks, inadequate organizational support.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Originality-Contributes to the literature by introducing a novel conceptualization of sustainable global careers, presenting a multi-level framework that integrates individual agency, organizational support, societal considerations. By combining sustainable career theory with AMO theory, global mobility perspectives, it provides a holistic approach for understanding, managing international career sustainability, offering new theoretical insights, practical guidance for HR professionals and organizations managing global talent.
</div>

As frontier AI systems, including large language models and generative image tools, become embedded in global digital infrastructure, their capacity to interpret human appearance raises critical safety and ethical concerns.&nbsp;
<div>
 This paper presents a case study based on an interaction with an AI system (Grok), prompted to “fix” a human face and generate a “normal version.” The system complied without reflection, producing an edited image in which visible differences were removed and replaced with a statistically typical facial representation.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 This response reveals a systemic failure mode, defined here as Algorithmic Homogenization: the tendency of AI systems to converge diverse human appearances toward a narrow aesthetic or statistical norm. In this process, visible differences are implicitly treated as deviations to be corrected rather than valid forms of human variation.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The paper argues that this behavior constitutes a meaningful AI safety concern. When systems interpret visible difference as noise rather than data, they risk reinforcing digital lookism, reducing representational diversity, and contributing to psychosocial harm.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 This paper introduces Appearance Intelligence as a framework for ensuring that AI systems can interpret and represent human appearance without defaulting to erasure or normative bias.&nbsp;
</div>

The rapid growth of ultra-short maturity equity index options, particularly same-day (0DTE) contracts on the S&amp;P 500 index, has revealed a striking empirical feature: the implied volatility term structure across adjacent expiries is often highly irregular and oscillatory. Standard stochastic volatility models typically produce smooth short-maturity volatility curves and therefore struggle to match these patterns without introducing maturity-specific adjustments.&nbsp; 
<div>
 This paper proposes a different explanation. We argue that a substantial portion of the observed irregularity arises from incorrect measurement of time rather than from exotic volatility dynamics. Option prices identify total variance, which depends on the amount of time remaining until maturity. If the clock used to measure time does not match the rate at which variance accumulates in the market, implied volatilities inferred from option prices exhibit artificial oscillations across maturities.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 We introduce a simple scale-free metric, the oscillation index, that quantifies local zig-zag behavior of the implied volatility term structure. Using this index, we conduct a horse race between several time conventions: calendar time, business time, and a stochastic clock constructed from high-frequency volatility patterns in E-mini S&amp;P 500 futures.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 We show that a substantial portion of the apparent irregularity in the ultra-short implied volatility term structure is driven by the measurement of time. A stochastic clock calibrated to intraday volatility patterns produces substantially smoother term structures than both calendar time and a business-time clock, yielding the lowest oscillation index on 96% of trading days. In contrast, the calendar clock never produces the smoothest term structure. These results suggest that much of the observed "noise" in short-maturity implied volatility reflects mismeasured time rather than complex volatility dynamics.
</div>

We identify and study a new structural class of allocation problems, adversarial procurement in two-value space: a mission-driven, budget-constrained buyer faces an active rival whose valuation aligns with the buyer's acquisition price, so price is both a cost and a signal of rival removal risk. Conservation siting provides the most transparent instance; the same structure arises in other domains.&nbsp; 
<div>
 Two lessons emerge from computational, formal, and empirical analysis. First, rivalry changes the meaning of threat: when rivals re-target after being blocked, threat-targeting is fragile, and constraining re-targeting can paradoxically reduce realised conservation gains. Second, two-value space changes the meaning of cost: a value-first rule dominates the textbook cost-effectiveness ratio-a pattern we call the knapsack reversal-because the ratio rule defers contested high-value items on cost-effectiveness grounds while the rival takes them by virtue of their high cost.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 In the pure rivalry case without budget constraints, a distribution-free theorem characterises the mechanism: value-first weakly dominates ratio-greedy pathwise on every generic finite board against the rival (Theorem 5.1). When budgets are introduced, theorems establish asymptotic dominance in benchmark i.i.d. cases at independence and at ρ = 0.3-where the gap is larger, not smaller, than at independence (Theorems 5.2 and 5.3). Subgame-perfect-equilibrium computations across 17 distribution classes confirm the same monotonicity under SPE Farmer best response.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 A case study on Bolivia (390 planning units, ρ ≈ 0.29) reproduces the qualitative rankings while revealing a 15-20% "disappointment gap" between static cost-effectiveness planning benchmarks and realised adversarial outcomes. The findings imply that prevailing additionality-targeting and cost-effectiveness benchmarks systematically overstate realisable conservation gains under contested land allocation.&nbsp;
</div>
